    {
      "text": "5.3. Insights: the difﬁculty of reachability analysis and algorithmic design",
      "bbox": [
        89.99998474121094,
        341.99969482421875,
        434.1274719238281,
        351.8069763183594
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Theorem 2 reveals which characteristics of the problem make reachability analysis challenging:",
      "bbox": [
        89.99998474121094,
        361.4396057128906,
        507.2400817871094,
        371.19232177734375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "• Assuming the smoothness of f is necessary: given an input set X and a sampling distribution",
      "bbox": [
        93.48298645019531,
        378.869140625,
        522.0006103515625,
        389.4073181152344
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "PX , one can construct problems for which sampling-based reachability analysis algorithms re-",
      "bbox": [
        102.7559814453125,
        393.20361328125,
        521.9962158203125,
        404.64532470703125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "quire arbitrarily many samples to compute an ϵ-accurate approximation of Y, see Section 6.1. To",
      "bbox": [
        102.7559814453125,
        405.9671630859375,
        521.9999389648438,
        416.5053405761719
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "derive ﬁnite-sample rates, assuming that the reachability map f is L-Lipschitz (Assumption 2) is",
      "bbox": [
        102.7559814453125,
        420.1280517578125,
        522.00244140625,
        430.0553283691406
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "necessary if only assumptions on input coverage density (Assumption 3) are available.",
      "bbox": [
        102.75595092773438,
        433.85162353515625,
        479.4578857421875,
        443.6043395996094
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "• The smoother the easier: a smaller Lipschitz constant L and a larger radius parameter r induce",
      "bbox": [
        93.48294830322266,
        450.84906005859375,
        522.0006103515625,
        460.7763366699219
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "tighter bounds in Theorem 2, requiring a smaller number of samples M to obtain a desired",
      "bbox": [
        102.75595092773438,
        464.3980712890625,
        522.002685546875,
        474.3253479003906
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "accuracy with high probability 1 −δM. Indeed, such conditions guarantee a lower bound on the",
      "bbox": [
        102.75595092773438,
        477.336181640625,
        521.9984741210938,
        488.7532043457031
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "probability of sampling outputs yi = f(xi) ∈Y that are close to the boundary ∂Y, which is",
      "bbox": [
        102.75596618652344,
        490.88616943359375,
        522.0028686523438,
        502.8203430175781
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "necessary to accurately reconstruct the true convex hull of the reachable set from samples.",
      "bbox": [
        102.75601196289062,
        505.22064208984375,
        495.821533203125,
        514.973388671875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "• Scalability: by Theorem 2, the number of required samples to reach a desired ϵ-accuracy with",
      "bbox": [
        93.4830093383789,
        522.2180786132812,
        521.996337890625,
        532.1453857421875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "high probability depends on the covering number. This constant characterizes the size of the pa-",
      "bbox": [
        102.75601196289062,
        535.941650390625,
        522.0034790039062,
        545.6943969726562
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "rameter space in terms of dimensionality (the number of different parameters) and volume (vari-",
      "bbox": [
        102.75601196289062,
        549.4906005859375,
        522.0034790039062,
        559.2433471679688
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "ations of each parameter). Given any X ∈K and d = supx∈∂X ∥x∥, a simple and general bound",
      "bbox": [
        102.75601196289062,
        562.2551879882812,
        522.00244140625,
        575.1724243164062
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "for the covering number is D(∂X, ϵ) ≤(2d√n/ϵ)n (Shalev-Shwartz and Ben-David, 2009).",
      "bbox": [
        102.7559814453125,
        567.9461669921875,
        506.022216796875,
        586.3423461914062
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
